    impact: "If you're running on a short-sleep routine, a mid-afternoon nap can reliably improve how you feel (more positive mood, less subjective sleepiness) for the next few hours. For learning, the data point to ~30 minutes of sleep as the best trade-off: it boosted later recognition without the longer nap's extra practicality costs, and any sleep inertia effects faded by ~30 minutes post-wake.",
    constraints: "Small sample size and a specific population (young adults with habitual short sleep) limit generalizability. The vigilance task was short (3-min PVT), which may reduce sensitivity. The study did not directly test how these naps affect the following night's sleep, and the memory delay window was fixed.",
