    summary: "This study analyzed U.S. NHANES data and found that higher intake of high-quality carbohydrates was linked to lower odds of poor sleep patterns. Higher intake of low-quality carbohydrates, added sugar in particular, was linked to higher odds of poor sleep patterns. The most favorable pattern was eating more whole-food carbs (like whole grains, fruits, and non-starchy vegetables) while keeping refined carbs and added sugars lower.",
    executionProtocol: [
      { title: "Sample", desc: "Researchers selected adults from four NHANES cycles and excluded people with incomplete diet or sleep data." },
      { title: "Measure", desc: "They estimated carb intake using two 24-hour diet recalls and split carbs into high-quality vs low-quality sources." },
      { title: "Score", desc: "They scored sleep using sleep duration, snoring frequency, and daytime sleepiness to label healthy vs poor sleep patterns." },
      { title: "Model", desc: "They ran survey-weighted logistic regression models to test associations while adjusting for many health and lifestyle factors." }
    ],
    researchObjective: "To test how the quality and amount of daily carbohydrate intake relates to poor sleep patterns in U.S. adults.",
    methodology: {
      intro: "This is a cross-sectional analysis of NHANES data using survey-weighted regression to link carbohydrate intake patterns with a combined sleep score.",
      points: [
        "Diet was measured using the average of two 24-hour recalls and mapped to food-pattern components.",
        "High-quality carbs were defined mainly as whole grains, legumes, whole fruits, and non-starchy vegetables, while low-quality carbs included refined grains, fruit juice, starchy vegetables, and added sugars.",
        "Sleep pattern was scored from three self-reported behaviors: short/long sleep duration, snoring, and daytime sleepiness."
      ]
    },
    observations: [
      "Higher high-quality carb intake was linked to lower odds of poor sleep patterns (reported OR about 0.71 in the fully adjusted model).",
      "Higher low-quality carb intake and higher total carbs were linked to higher odds of poor sleep patterns (reported OR about 1.39 and 1.31, respectively, for the highest vs lowest groups).",
      "People who ate more high-quality carbs and less low-quality carbs had meaningfully lower risk of poor sleep patterns compared to the opposite pattern (reported OR about 0.64)."
    ],
    impact: "If you want to use carbs to support sleep, this study points toward shifting your carb sources, not just cutting carbs. Prioritize whole grains, whole fruits, and non-starchy vegetables, and try to reduce added sugars and refined carbs, since those were most consistently tied to worse sleep. It also suggests that very high overall carb intake may be a problem when that extra intake is mostly low-quality carbs.",
    constraints: "This is cross-sectional, so it cannot prove that carbs cause better or worse sleep. Diet and sleep were self-reported, which can be inaccurate. The study also did not test meal timing, so it cannot answer whether carbs at night help or hurt sleep for a given person.",
