    observations: [
      "Relaxation increased significantly after the silence period.",
      "Mood improved slightly but significantly after the silence period.",
      "Emotional arousal decreased significantly after the silence period.",
      "Boredom ratings were low overall, and most people felt the time passed quickly."
    ],
    impact: "This suggests that even a short, device-free silence break can calm people down and improve mood, at least in certain groups. It also challenges the idea that being alone with your thoughts is naturally unpleasant, and points to context, expectations, and participant background as major factors. Practically, it supports simple 'silence breaks' as a low-effort stress reset during school days.",
    constraints: "The sample was not representative and skewed toward students in education and social programs, which may relate to comfort with introspection. The setting was 'quiet' but not absolute silence, and outcomes rely on self-report. The study did not compare silence directly against other activities like reading or phone use in the same experiment.",
    source: {
      title: "'Just Think'—Students Feel Significantly More Relaxed, Less Aroused, and in a Better Mood after a Period of Silence Alone in a Room",
      doi: "10.3390/psych1010024",
      link: "https://www.mdpi.com/2624-8611/1/1/24"
    }
