    summary: "25 healthy young women did a threat-and-uncertainty task while doing either fast breathing (2 seconds in, 2 seconds out) or slow breathing (6 seconds in, 6 seconds out). Slow breathing led to lower negative feelings and lower arousal, especially when the situation felt uncertain. Brain and heart signals also shifted during slow breathing in ways that matched a calmer state.",
