    researchObjective: "To update and simplify how we understand brain fluid circulation by combining old and new evidence and proposing a better model for clinical imaging in humans.",
    methodology: {
      intro: "This is a narrative review that synthesizes prior research and clinical imaging work to propose an updated framework.",
      points: [
        "They summarized major historical discoveries and how ideas about brain fluid flow changed over time.",
        "They reviewed animal experiments and modern imaging studies that track fluid movement in and out of the brain.",
        "They compared human vs mouse anatomy and outlined what future MRI studies should measure in real patients."
      ]
    },
    observations: [
      "The brain does not have classic lymph vessels inside the brain tissue, but it still moves fluid and clears waste through organized pathways in and around the brain.",
      "Modern evidence suggests brain fluid flow is mixed, flexible, and can move through multiple routes, not just one simple direction.",
      "Human imaging studies support fluid movement into brain tissue and drainage toward lymph nodes, but the exact routes are still hard to map with today's MRI resolution."
    ],
    impact: "If doctors can measure these fluid pathways better, it could improve how we track brain aging, swelling, inflammation, and diseases like dementia, and it may open new ways to deliver drugs or support brain waste clearance.",
    constraints: "This paper is a review and model update, not a single new experiment. Many conclusions depend on combining studies that use different methods, and some small fluid pathways in humans are still too tiny to see clearly with standard MRI.",
