    summary: "Researchers analyzed 358 different methods of measuring how willing people are to take risks, using data from over 579,000 people tracked over time. They found that different measurement methods give different results and don't consistently agree with each other. Self-reported willingness to take risks stayed more stable over time than actual risk-taking behavior in tests, and age and life domain played important roles in how consistent these measures were.",
