    impact: "In everyday terms, eyes act like a volume knob for facial expressions. If the eyes look smaller, faces may come across as less attractive regardless of the emotion. If the eyes look larger, expressions can feel stronger or more noticeable, which may shape first impressions in photos, video calls, and media where eye emphasis is common (lighting, makeup, camera angle, or filters).",
    constraints: "The study used only women's faces and digitally changed eye size by 15%, which may look less natural and could affect results. The participant sample was relatively small and not balanced by gender, and the dataset was not publicly available due to privacy restrictions. The findings describe perception in an image-rating task, not real-life interactions.",
